    "kind": "info",
    "title_en": "Tone marks count from today",
    "body_en": "Most of yesterday's pinyin came without tone marks (you for yǒu, nuer for nǚ'ér). Without marks, \"you\" could be yǒu (to have) or yòu (again), so from today answers without tone marks earn half credit. Tip: on most pinyin keyboards you type ü as v.",
    "body_py": "Guānyú shēngdiào: zuótiān nǐ de pīnyīn dàduō méiyǒu shēngdiào fúhào. Méiyǒu fúhào, \"you\" kěyǐ shì yǒu, yě kěyǐ shì yòu. Suǒyǐ cóng jīntiān kāishǐ, méiyǒu shēngdiào fúhào de dá'àn zhǐ dé yíbàn fēn. Xiǎo tíshì: zài pīnyīn jiànpán shàng, ü dǎ \"v\"."
  },
  "teach": {
    "intro": {
      "en": "Hi Evan! Yesterday you learned to say what you have, like \"I have two kids. | Wǒ yǒu liǎng gè háizi.\" Today we learn to point at people and ask \"Who is this?\" It's the perfect sentence for flipping through family photos with your kids.",
      "py": "Nǐ hǎo, Evan! Zuótiān nǐ xuéle zěnme shuō nǐ yǒu shénme. Jīntiān wǒmen xué zěnme zhǐzhe rén wèn: \"Zhè shì shéi?\" Gēn háizimen yìqǐ kàn jiātíng zhàopiàn de shíhou, zhège jùzi fēicháng hǎoyòng."
    },
